{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T11:37:14.169Z","ordinal":69534,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call","id":"ctc_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac62ee295d887d0806ac678b2909cd4","status":"completed","call_id":"call_Nix7dZjpDPBbN9tOb6SoRHEe","name":"exec","input":"text(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"python3 - <<'PY'\\ns=open('/private/tmp/dev79-sdk-policy-probe-d.mjs').read().replace(\\\"['plain-close','signal-close','signal-retry-deny','signal-dnt-start','signal-batch-deny']\\\",\\\"['plain-retry-deny','plain-batch-deny']\\\").replace(\\\"mode==='signal-retry-deny'\\\",\\\"mode.endsWith('retry-deny')\\\").replace(\\\"mode==='signal-batch-deny'\\\",\\\"mode.endsWith('batch-deny')\\\")\\ns=s.replace(\\\"wire.push({url:\\\",\\\"wire.push({browserPolicy:await page.evaluate(()=>({dnt:navigator.doNotTrack,closed:window.fixtureClosed})),url:\\\")\\ns=s.replace('dev79-sdk-policy-probe-d.json','dev79-sdk-policy-probe-e.json');open('/private/tmp/dev79-sdk-policy-probe-e.mjs','w').write(s)\\nPY\\nPLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH=/private/tmp/taxkit-local-toolchain/ms-playwright /Users/cooper/.nvm/versions/node/v24.16.0/bin/node /private/tmp/dev79-sdk-policy-probe-e.mjs\",\"yield_time_ms\":1000,\"max_output_tokens\":1000}));\ntext(await tools.exec_command({cmd:\"sed -n '1,280p' apps/web/src/lib/runtime.server.ts; sed -n '1,250p' apps/web/src/worker.ts; cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/effect/posthog-analytics.md; cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/posthog.md\",\"workdir\":\"/Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit\",\"max_output_tokens\":5500}));\n","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","create_time":1791372971.345854}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T11:37:14.196Z","ordinal":69535,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","session_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","root_turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","response_id":"resp_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac62eab08bc87d08f581d6e0fe6f44a","usage":{"input_tokens":175258,"cached_input_tokens":173568,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":1685,"reasoning_output_tokens":1306,"total_tokens":176943},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":64823255,"cached_input_tokens":63517440,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":317212,"reasoning_output_tokens":124089,"total_tokens":65140467},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1301333457,"cached_input_tokens":1274540928,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6455274,"reasoning_output_tokens":2873926,"total_tokens":1307788731}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T11:37:15.402Z","ordinal":69536,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-ace80bfc-5d1a-40f8-974a-81edeeaaf1f0","process_id":"85514","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,280p' apps/web/src/lib/runtime.server.ts; sed -n '1,250p' apps/web/src/worker.ts; cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/effect/posthog-analytics.md; cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/posthog.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,280p' apps/web/src/lib/runtime.server.ts","name":"runtime.server.ts","path":"apps/web/src/lib/runtime.server.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,250p' apps/web/src/worker.ts","name":"worker.ts","path":"apps/web/src/worker.ts"},{"type":"read","cmd":"cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/effect/posthog-analytics.md","name":"posthog-analytics.md","path":".agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/effect/posthog-analytics.md"},{"type":"read","cmd":"cat .agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/posthog.md","name":"posthog.md","path":".agents/skills/alchemy-iac/references/providers/posthog.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"import \"@tanstack/react-start/server-only\";\nimport { CalculatorHostTelemetryLive } from \"@taxkit/api-rpc/host-telemetry\";\nimport { env } from \"cloudflare:workers\";\nimport { ConfigProvider, Layer, ManagedRuntime } from \"effect\";\n\nimport { WebsiteServerLive } from \"./live.server.layer\";\n\n// One server runner per loaded Worker module. Each incoming server function\n// supplies its request signal; no request constructs a Layer or another runner.\nexport const appRuntime = ManagedRuntime.make(\n  Layer.mergeAll(\n    WebsiteServerLive(env.TAXKIT_API).pipe(\n      Layer.provide(ConfigProvider.layer(ConfigProvider.fromUnknown(env)))\n    ),\n    CalculatorHostTelemetryLive(\"website\")\n  )\n);\nsed: apps/web/src/worker.ts: No such file or directory\n# PostHog analytics in a separate Website and API\n\nUse this when adding browser and backend analytics to the repository pattern.\nThis is an optional capability, not part of every generated scaffold. Start from\nthe questions, collection policy and accepted loss budget, then follow the\n[Alchemy resource guide](../providers/posthog.md) and\n[Effect browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md).\nKeep product analytics separate from request logs, traces and operational metrics.\nApply the sibling [strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to\nschemas, services, adapters, infrastructure and tests; the SDK does not grant a\nwhole analytics folder an exception.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Put each responsibility with its owner](#put-each-responsibility-with-its-owner)\n- [Make the Effect contract reviewable](#make-the-effect-contract-reviewable)\n- [Count backend work separately](#count-backend-work-separately)\n- [Keep a browser relay on the owning Website](#keep-a-browser-relay-on-the-owning-website)\n- [Verify the assembled capability](#verify-the-assembled-capability)\n\n## Put each responsibility with its owner\n\nAdapt these paths to the target repository; they are an ownership map rather\nthan a requirement to create every file:\n\n```text\npackages/analytics/src/\n  schemas.ts                 checked settings, event vocabulary and brands\n  errors.ts                  closed capture failures\n  service.ts                 named browser/backend operations\n  live.layer.ts              native backend HTTP capture\n  test.layer.ts              deterministic service implementation\npackages/infrastructure/src/posthog/\n  schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n  management.adapter.layer.ts  private Distilled operations and credentials\n  resources.ts / dashboards.ts  resource lifecycle and desired chart catalogue\n  bindings.egress.ts            checked stage-specific deployment outputs\napps/web/src/lib/analytics/\n  browser.adapter.layer.ts   private SDK lifecycle\n  capture.consumer.ts        synchronous checked before-send callback\n  relay/\n    schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n    live.layer.ts            one native outbound HTTP attempt\n    body.ingress.ts          bounded byte reader\n    request.ingress.ts       checked Website HTTP request\n    response.egress.ts       native response adaptation\napps/api/src/\n  analytics.layer.ts         request-scoped collection and finalisation\nalchemy.posthog.run.ts       composition-only resource stack\n```\n\nPackages import explicit package exports and never app internals. The existing\nserver runtime composes the relay service; the React-owned Atom runtime composes\nthe browser service. The native API entry composes backend capture with its\nincoming request Effect fibre. No analytics package starts a runtime or shares\nmutable per-request state across users.\n\n## Make the Effect contract reviewable\n\n- Public operations return `Effect.Effect<Success, AnalyticsError, never>` with\n  Schema-derived inputs and outputs. Capture transport, configuration and SDK\n  dependencies in the Layer; typecheck the real implementation against\n  `Service.of`. Native request handlers may retain documented host requirements;\n  those requirements must not leak into the shared analytics service.\n- Use named `Effect.fn(\"Service.operation\")` with a generator directly for\n  sequential public work. Use `Effect.fnUntraced` for reusable private work that\n  belongs to the caller's span. Keep pure total transformations plain. Split\n  files by schema/error/service/transport ownership, not one helper per SDK call.\n- Decode stage, host, token and collection mode with their owning Schema.\n  Model enabled/disabled settings as a closed union: disabled needs no fake key\n  or placeholder host. Invalid enabled settings are a named configuration\n  failure, not configured-off. If startup must remain available, make the\n  downgrade a reported policy at composition, separate from deliberate disable.\n- Decode external absence to `Option` once and prefer `Option.match` for both\n  branches. Do not access `.value`, inspect `_tag` or use unsafe extraction.\n  Use `Boolean.match` for boolean choices and `Match.exhaustive` for closed\n  states; do not introduce Option solely to hide a boolean decision. Return\n  nullable values only at a host contract that explicitly requires them.\n- Decode incoming data and encode owned outgoing payloads in their ingress and\n  egress owners. No assertions, raw JSON, raw fetch, ambient environment reads,\n  clocks, timers, random globals or raw Promise orchestration. The browser's\n  named SDK adapter and synchronous consumer are the documented host exceptions;\n  backend HttpClient and Distilled calls remain native Effect.\n- Map expected SDK/HTTP/codec failures to closed Schema-tagged errors with safe\n  operation/reason fields. Best-effort capture catches those named errors at\n  its application owner. Do not erase configuration, permission or decode\n  failure with blanket `Effect.option`/`Effect.ignore`, convert it with `orDie`,\n  or catch all Causes to report success. Preserve interruption and defects.\n  Discarding an invalid optional campaign field is a separate, explicit policy.\n- Create `Ref`s and any concurrency limits during the intended Layer/request\n  lifetime. Use scoped acquisition and bounded finalisers. No mutable module\n  client, request accumulator, per-click runner or detached fibre. Logging and\n  span fields use safe fixed names; event bodies and visitor IDs stay out.\n\n## Count backend work separately\n\nCollect a bounded set of domain outcomes in a request-local `Ref`, then send one\nrequest-completed event and those outcomes together. Define whether health,\npreflight, discovery and streamed responses count. Pick a truthful completion\npoint for streams and cancellation; do not count a stream twice or claim the\nbody was delivered merely because response headers exist.\n\nUse native Effect `HttpClient` for capture rather than adding a server SDK solely\nto send batches. Check and encode the owned wire Schema; distinguish accepted\nHTTP responses, rejection and transport failure. Bound the entire send and keep\nresources scoped. Use Effect Clock/Random for timing and event identity; do not\nintroduce ambient clocks or random globals. Worker CPU timing can differ from\nprovider-measured latency, so name the recorded duration accurately.\n\nOn native Alchemy Cloudflare Workers, use the installed\n`WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)` API for bounded background sends.\nIt extends request work and preserves the native Effect context without a new\nrunner in owned code. Call it while the native request context is valid. The\ninstalled Alchemy implementation captures the calling services; it does not\nextend every service's acquisition scope. Close over only dependencies that\nremain alive for the send and open the HTTP send scope inside the background\nEffect. Do not carry a soon-to-close request scope or raw host context into a\nsecond runtime. Test request finalisation while the controlled send is pending.\nIt is not a persistent queue or a promise of delivery after termination.\nExpected analytics failure should not change the application's response when\nbest-effort loss is the agreed policy. Keep fixed safe diagnostics and separate\nbackend request IDs from browser visitor IDs. Do not add retries without deciding\nhow duplicates are prevented and how long sends may live.\n\n## Keep a browser relay on the owning Website\n\nFor a minimal browser client, an existing Website path such as `/ingest/e/` avoids\nanother Worker and cross-origin delivery to `api.<domain>`. A thin TanStack route\ncalls a native Effect HTTP program through the existing server runner. This is\nfixed provider ingress for the Website's browser client, not a domain API or a\nsecond Content/HTTP/RPC implementation. It is the narrow exception to keeping\nbackend machine routes out of the Website. A separate proxy Worker or API route\nis valid when isolation or other requirements justify it; decide that explicitly.\n\nSet the browser SDK's checked `api_host` to the Website path. Backend capture\ncan stay direct to its fixed regional ingestion host. The relay reduces direct\nanalytics-domain blocking even for explicit events; it cannot recover events\nthat never reach it or defeat every blocker. Preserve chosen privacy controls.\n\nQualify the pinned SDK's actual endpoint, media types, compression, query keys,\nbatch delay, retry policy and response handling. The source-derived SDK used\n`/e/`, roughly three-second batches, gzip bytes labelled `text/plain`, plus JSON\nand form fallbacks. Those are version-specific observations, not universal\nPostHog endpoints. Enabling flags, replay or external scripts introduces paths\nthat this minimal relay deliberately does not support; revisit the protocol\nbefore enabling them. Do not proxy arbitrary URLs or guessed assets.\n\nKeep the relay small but check its real boundary:\n\n- Fixed POST path, fixed regional upstream and allowed Website origin. Origin\n  checking is a browser policy, not authentication for a public project token.\n- Checked retry/compression query, rejecting duplicates and unknown keys.\n  Use Schema optional-to-Option codecs and `Option.match`; encode query keys\n  only at the outgoing boundary.\n- Unchanged bytes, UUIDs and timestamps. Do not parse and rebuild SDK batches\n  merely to forward them. The owning Schema checks protocol metadata and bounded\n  bytes; the browser consumer separately owns allowed event properties.\n- Native `Stream.limitBytes` bounds both request and reply even without\n  Content-Length. Supply a typed failing fallback when the limit is crossed:\n  `Stream.empty` would silently accept a truncated body. Test the exact limit\n  and the crossing chunk; never forward a partially collected oversized batch.\n  No manual mutable byte counter. Compressed-byte limits do not establish\n  decompressed event size; the forwarding relay does not decompress.\n- One scoped deadline covers request body, upstream headers and reply body.\n  The source pattern chose 1 MiB per body and five seconds; choose limits for the\n  target application's actual payloads and runtime constraints.\n- Rebuild allowed headers. Strip caller cookies, auth, IP forwarding and trace\n  headers; disable native HttpClient trace propagation. Configure the native\n  transport for manual redirects before sending, then reject every 3xx response\n  without forwarding its Location. Rejecting a final status is insufficient if\n  the transport has already followed it. In the qualified FetchHttpClient,\n  `RequestInit` is read at execution and its Layer uses `layerMergedContext` to\n  carry acquisition settings into requests. Providing manual redirects to that\n  Layer is supported; verify the actual call retains that setting and no caller\n  overrides it. Do not add `followRedirects`.\n- Preserve upstream success/error statuses and checked retry headers; return\n  `no-store`. Do not report a failed provider attempt as successful delivery.\n\nKeep SDK retries as the single retry owner for this design. Forward each attempt\nonce with no relay retry, durable queue or direct-send fallback unless explicitly\ndesigned. Browser batching still leaves a loss window on tab closure. Give relay\nrequests their own telemetry category so they do not inflate API/domain counts.\n\n## Verify the assembled capability\n\nTypecheck live and test implementations at the same closed service contract.\nCheck the error channel and Layer input/output types; a signature made clean by\nan assertion or `orDie` is not proof. Use `@effect/vitest` for Effect tests and\nEffect FileSystem/ChildProcess for test work; no raw async setup or real sleeps.\n\nUse the actual lint command and negative fixtures to reject SDK access outside\nthe browser adapter, Promise bridges outside named host adapters, misplaced\ncodecs, manual tag checks and unchecked optional extraction. Split large files\nby their schemas/service/error/transport owners; do not create forwarding helpers.\n\nTest unchanged bytes and supported formats, status handling, stripped headers,\nexact/oversized bodies without Content-Length, a stalled incoming body, late\nheaders followed by a stalled reply, interruption and resource cleanup. Native\nHttpClient may represent a bodyless response as a typed empty-body error; qualify\nand handle that case without masking other stream failures. Prove one upstream\nattempt and closed service requirements with deterministic Layers/TestClock.\nTest redirect refusal with a real local native transport as well as a fake\nHttpClient: a fake returning 302 cannot prove the real client never followed it.\nCover late completion after timeout, concurrent requests with separate state,\nrequest-scope closure during background sending and safe drop diagnostics.\n\nReject an implementation until enabled/disabled configuration, each named error,\nSchema round-trips, lifecycle, concurrency and the actual lint prohibitions have\nbeen checked. Missing live delivery evidence must remain a stated non-claim.\n\nThen follow the resource guide's delivery sequence: controlled enabled Preview,\nfresh browser requests, exact stored UUIDs and backend events, temporary domain\nblocking and client retries when relevant, restored privacy overrides and disabled\nordinary Preview, and separately authorised production plan/apply/readback.\nLocal tests, HTTP 200, provider state and stored-event queries are separate proof.\nKeep a dated receipt in the application repository; do not copy its account IDs,\npersonal details, tokens or deployment-specific findings into reusable skills.\n\nSee [PostHog proxy guidance](https://posthog.com/docs/advanced/proxy) and\n[native Alchemy Worker source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/tree/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers).\n# PostHog resources with native Alchemy\n\nUse this when adding PostHog to an Effect application or maintaining its projects,\ndashboards, insights and dashboard tiles. Management and event collection have\ndifferent owners and credentials. Read the sibling\n[application layout](../effect/posthog-analytics.md)\nand [browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md)\nfor the runtime side. Apply the sibling\n[strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to the management\nservice, provider callbacks and tests as well as the application.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Establish the intended collection](#establish-the-intended-collection)\n- [Separate resource management from capture](#separate-resource-management-from-capture)\n- [Declare durable resource ownership](#declare-durable-resource-ownership)\n- [Connect deployment outputs to the applications](#connect-deployment-outputs-to-the-applications)\n- [Prove each claim separately](#prove-each-claim-separately)\n- [Primary sources](#primary-sources)\n\n## Establish the intended collection\n\nAgree the questions and selected events before declaring charts. Keep browser\npage views, chosen actions, backend requests and completed domain operations\nseparate. A server request is not a visit, and retries are not new user actions.\nDecide collection controls, identity, allowed properties, retention, environment\nseparation and accepted event loss. Explicit events with automatic capture and\nrecordings off are a useful starting point, not a universal product requirement.\nConsent is an application decision; never infer its legal requirements from\nthe application's country or copy another application's choice.\n\nResolve the authorised account, organisation, project region and project limit.\nUse the existing secret store. Choose management-key capabilities from the actual\noperations: project/dashboard/insight management and query readback are distinct\nfrom billing. Billing and spending limits remain separate unless deliberately\nowned; inspect each product's limit instead of assuming one account-wide cap.\nStop an apply rejected by entitlement rather than retrying the same creates.\n\nInspect installed Alchemy, Effect and `@distilled.cloud/posthog` types and locked\nupstream references. The source-derived pattern was qualified with Effect\n`4.0.0-rc.117`, Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, Distilled `1.0.0-rc.12` and browser SDK\n`1.435.6`. This is a historical compatibility snapshot, not a current-version\nrecommendation. Verify it again for the target repository. Do not copy patches\nor internal SDK endpoints without proving the installed contract.\n\n## Separate resource management from capture\n\n| Owner                                            | Work                               | Credential                                    |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Native Alchemy stack and private Distilled Layer | Project/chart lifecycle            | Redacted management key, deployment only      |\n| Checked runtime configuration                    | Matching project, region and stage | Public project token; omit management details |\n| Browser Effect adapter                           | Selected browser events            | Matching public project token                 |\n| Backend Effect HttpClient Layer                  | Checked event batches              | Matching public project token                 |\n\nDistilled's management API is not the browser event SDK or a capture client.\nIts operations already return Effect: do not wrap them in Promise adapters.\nKeep raw operations, credentials and provider payloads inside\n`management.adapter.layer.ts`. Public service operations take branded addresses\nand return checked application-owned state. Decode results immediately; map\nexpected failures to safe named errors. Never let a raw provider response or\nmanagement key reach a Worker, root loader, browser bundle or error log.\n\nThe service exposes closed `Effect.Effect<Success, PostHogManagementError, never>`\noperations implemented with named `Effect.fn`; acquisition failures stay in the\nLayer error channel. Use Schema-backed Config and `Redacted` for credentials.\nNo raw client accessor, generic request/callback or primitive address strings.\nDo not erase provider errors with `Effect.option` or `orDie`. Model only genuine\nnot-found as `Option.none()` and branch with `Option.match`. Expected errors\ncarry bounded safe codes/reasons, not raw responses, SDK errors or arbitrary\nCauses. Distilled's typed transport still needs application boundary decoding.\n\n## Declare durable resource ownership\n\nUse native provider Resources if the installed Alchemy version supplies the\nneeded lifecycle. Otherwise implement small custom Resources and Providers for\nprojects, dashboards, insights and tiles. Follow\n[custom resource semantics](custom-resources.md); native Alchemy CLI remains\nthe only plan/apply/destroy owner. Do not create a second provider-write script.\n\nKeep checked definitions in schemas, a dashboard catalogue in its own owner,\nthe semantic management service separate from its live adapter, and lifecycle\nproviders separate from the root graph. Add further files only for real owners.\nThe root composes Layers, state, configuration and named resource declarations.\nResource definitions and stored outputs use Schema-derived branded identities\nwith actual format/range bounds; branding an unrestricted string is insufficient.\nDeclare known insight-query shapes and bounded catalogue values, not unchecked\nJSON or a generic `unknown` query payload. Keep deployment and runtime error\nvocabularies separate so provider-management details cannot leak into capture.\nUse outputs for project-dependent charts and tiles so the graph orders them.\n\nNormalise Alchemy's optional previous/output values to Option once inside each\nprovider callback and use `Option.match`; translate back only if the native\ncallback contract requires `undefined`. Preserve Alchemy's native Effect-returning\ncallback types instead of introducing a Promise runtime or unchecked cast.\nFor unresolved Outputs, follow the installed `Diff`/`Output` contract rather\nthan treating a deferred value as already decoded. A provider operation's\nrequired host context is not an excuse to expose it on management methods.\n\nUse stable logical resource IDs and exact ownership markers. A similar name,\nthe first search result or a project ID supplied by a foreign account does not\nprove ownership. Read an owned candidate and verify organisation/project/marker\nbefore mutation. Reject ambiguous matches. Do not automatically adopt a default\nproject. Ownership-field changes require an explicit replacement decision.\n\nList Schemas must describe all valid list items, including unrelated unnamed\ninsights and dashboards. Decode nullable/absent names to `Option`, then use\n`Option.match` to filter candidates. Apply stricter managed-name and ownership\nchecks only after selection. Follow every checked pagination page; a first-page\nmiss is not proof of absence. Check each page and bound traversal by total\noperation time, item/page limits and repeated-cursor detection. A limit or broken\npagination is a named inventory failure, not absence that permits creation.\nDo not fetch an unchecked next URL or forward credentials to another host.\nPreserve unrelated tile memberships when updating\na chart. A tile is an association with its own identity, not a duplicate insight.\n\nRead must distinguish missing, forbidden, invalid, transient and foreign data.\nDo not turn every failure into absence. Bound deadlines and retries with Effect;\nfor an uncertain create, read by ownership before any further write. An automatic\nretry of a non-idempotent create can duplicate projects or charts. Keep sensitive\nprovider bodies out of public failures and plan receipts.\n\nDeclare retention separately for each resource. Retaining a project preserves\nits event history and does not prevent updating or removing owned dashboards,\ninsights and tiles. Use each API's actual soft-delete semantics and check the\ndeleted flag on readback. Reviving a soft-deleted object needs an update even\nwhen desired fields are unchanged. Do not recreate a retained missing project\nsilently. Support the installed CLI's bulk-destroy path as well as ordinary\nremoval; test both against retained projects and foreign objects.\n\nA display rename from test to preview should preserve the existing logical ID,\nprovider ID, capture token and ownership marker. Existing configuration markers\ncan remain `test` if their checked contract requires it. New projects may use\nPreview naming from the start. Renaming is not permission to replace history.\n\n## Connect deployment outputs to the applications\n\nA dedicated analytics stack can own one production project and one shared\nnonproduction project. It need not create a project for every ephemeral PR.\nThe application stack reads the matching project through native references and\noutputs. Checked stage configuration selects production, deliberate Preview\ncollection or disabled collection; local and ordinary Preview collection should\nfollow the agreed policy. Reject cross-stage or cross-region combinations.\n\nEncode the owning configuration Schema once for each explicit Worker binding.\nKeep fallible validation/encoding in the typed provider or configuration owner\nbefore publishing outputs. In beta.79, `Output.mapEffect` requires an Effect\nwhose error type is `never`; it cannot carry an expected codec/configuration\nfailure. Use it only for genuinely infallible work, or select an already checked,\nencoded binding with pure `Output.map`. Do not add `orDie`, an assertion or an\nAction solely to hide that type restriction. Check the installed contract again\nfor another Alchemy version. Qualify Alchemy's runtime\nbinding representation: native environment handling can parse JSON before an\napplication JSON-string codec sees it. In beta.79, a native packed Redacted\nmarker preserved the encoded string; other versions require their own readback\ntest. Redaction avoids accidental logs, but a browser project token remains\npublic by design. Never distribute the whole deployment environment.\n\n## Prove each claim separately\n\n- Deterministic provider tests: create, no-op, update, uncertain-write recovery,\n  pagination, unnamed foreign items, ownership refusal, tile membership,\n  soft deletion and retained project removal. Include malformed responses,\n  permission refusal, incomplete/cyclic pagination, redacted errors, deadlines,\n  interrupted operations and cleanup. Typecheck live/test Layers against the\n  same closed contract; use native Effect test transport and `@effect/vitest`.\n- Native plan: exact revision/stage, expected create/update/remove actions,\n  capacity and account checks. Apply only within the user's authorised scope.\n- Provider readback: exact projects, privacy settings, private dashboards,\n  insight queries and tile membership. A successful apply is not this proof.\n- Runtime: matching bindings and real browser/backend sends on an isolated,\n  explicitly enabled Preview before production rollout.\n- Stored data: selected UUIDs counted once and separate backend event types.\n  A capture return or HTTP success does not prove storage.\n- Restoration: disable deliberate Preview capture through Alchemy, verify no\n  SDK load or sends in a fresh browser, and clean up ephemeral resources.\n\nKeep desired-state plans separate from provider drift. A no-change plan and a\nsuccessful drift command can coexist with reported provider differences. Record\nthem rather than repairing unrelated resources. A bounded receipt keeps source\nidentity, environment, plan/apply/readback and live checks separate; no secrets,\nvisitor identifiers, raw payloads or personal account details enter this skill.\n\n## Primary sources\n\nReconcile current documentation with installed types:\n[PostHog API](https://posthog.com/docs/api),\n[projects](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/projects),\n[capture](https://posthog.com/docs/api/capture),\n[privacy](https://posthog.com/docs/privacy),\n[Distilled source](https://github.com/distilled-cloud/distilled),\n[Alchemy source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy) and\n[Effect source](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect).\n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"import \"@tanstack/react-start/server-only\";\nimport { CalculatorHostTelemetryLive } from \"@taxkit/api-rpc/host-telemetry\";\nimport { env } from \"cloudflare:workers\";\nimport { ConfigProvider, Layer, ManagedRuntime } from \"effect\";\n\nimport { WebsiteServerLive } from \"./live.server.layer\";\n\n// One server runner per loaded Worker module. Each incoming server function\n// supplies its request signal; no request constructs a Layer or another runner.\nexport const appRuntime = ManagedRuntime.make(\n  Layer.mergeAll(\n    WebsiteServerLive(env.TAXKIT_API).pipe(\n      Layer.provide(ConfigProvider.layer(ConfigProvider.fromUnknown(env)))\n    ),\n    CalculatorHostTelemetryLive(\"website\")\n  )\n);\nsed: apps/web/src/worker.ts: No such file or directory\n# PostHog analytics in a separate Website and API\n\nUse this when adding browser and backend analytics to the repository pattern.\nThis is an optional capability, not part of every generated scaffold. Start from\nthe questions, collection policy and accepted loss budget, then follow the\n[Alchemy resource guide](../providers/posthog.md) and\n[Effect browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md).\nKeep product analytics separate from request logs, traces and operational metrics.\nApply the sibling [strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to\nschemas, services, adapters, infrastructure and tests; the SDK does not grant a\nwhole analytics folder an exception.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Put each responsibility with its owner](#put-each-responsibility-with-its-owner)\n- [Make the Effect contract reviewable](#make-the-effect-contract-reviewable)\n- [Count backend work separately](#count-backend-work-separately)\n- [Keep a browser relay on the owning Website](#keep-a-browser-relay-on-the-owning-website)\n- [Verify the assembled capability](#verify-the-assembled-capability)\n\n## Put each responsibility with its owner\n\nAdapt these paths to the target repository; they are an ownership map rather\nthan a requirement to create every file:\n\n```text\npackages/analytics/src/\n  schemas.ts                 checked settings, event vocabulary and brands\n  errors.ts                  closed capture failures\n  service.ts                 named browser/backend operations\n  live.layer.ts              native backend HTTP capture\n  test.layer.ts              deterministic service implementation\npackages/infrastructure/src/posthog/\n  schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n  management.adapter.layer.ts  private Distilled operations and credentials\n  resources.ts / dashboards.ts  resource lifecycle and desired chart catalogue\n  bindings.egress.ts            checked stage-specific deployment outputs\napps/web/src/lib/analytics/\n  browser.adapter.layer.ts   private SDK lifecycle\n  capture.consumer.ts        synchronous checked before-send callback\n  relay/\n    schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n    live.layer.ts            one native outbound HTTP attempt\n    body.ingress.ts          bounded byte reader\n    request.ingress.ts       checked Website HTTP request\n    response.egress.ts       native response adaptation\napps/api/src/\n  analytics.layer.ts         request-scoped collection and finalisation\nalchemy.posthog.run.ts       composition-only resource stack\n```\n\nPackages import explicit package exports and never app internals. The existing\nserver runtime composes the relay service; the React-owned Atom runtime composes\nthe browser service. The native API entry composes backend capture with its\nincoming request Effect fibre. No analytics package starts a runtime or shares\nmutable per-request state across users.\n\n## Make the Effect contract reviewable\n\n- Public operations return `Effect.Effect<Success, AnalyticsError, never>` with\n  Schema-derived inputs and outputs. Capture transport, configuration and SDK\n  dependencies in the Layer; typecheck the real implementation against\n  `Service.of`. Native request handlers may retain documented host requirements;\n  those requirements must not leak into the shared analytics service.\n- Use named `Effect.fn(\"Service.operation\")` with a generator directly for\n  sequential public work. Use `Effect.fnUntraced` for reusable private work that\n  belongs to the caller's span. Keep pure total transformations plain. Split\n  files by schema/error/service/transport ownership, not one helper per SDK call.\n- Decode stage, host, token and collection mode with their owning Schema.\n  Model enabled/disabled settings as a closed union: disabled needs no fake key\n  or placeholder host. Invalid enabled settings are a named configuration\n  failure, not configured-off. If startup must remain available, make the\n  downgrade a reported policy at composition, separate from deliberate disable.\n- Decode external absence to `Option` once and prefer `Option.match` for both\n  branches. Do not access `.value`, inspect `_tag` or use unsafe extraction.\n  Use `Boolean.match` for boolean choices and `Match.exhaustive` for closed\n  states; do not introduce Option solely to hide a boolean decision. Return\n  nullable values only at a host contract that explicitly requires them.\n- Decode incoming data and encode owned outgoing payloads in their ingress and\n  egress owners. No assertions, raw JSON, raw fetch, ambient environment reads,\n  clocks, timers, random globals or raw Promise orchestration. The browser's\n  named SDK adapter and synchronous consumer are the documented host exceptions;\n  backend HttpClient and Distilled calls remain native Effect.\n- Map expected SDK/HTTP/codec failures to closed Schema-tagged errors with safe\n  operation/reason fields. Best-effort capture catches those named errors at\n  its application owner. Do not erase configuration, permission or decode\n  failure with blanket `Effect.option`/`Effect.ignore`, convert it with `orDie`,\n  or catch all Causes to report success. Preserve interruption and defects.\n  Discarding an invalid optional campaign field is a separate, explicit policy.\n- Create `Ref`s and any concurrency limits during the intended Layer/request\n  lifetime. Use scoped acquisition and bounded finalisers. No mutable module\n  client, request accumulator, per-click runner or detached fibre. Logging and\n  span fields use safe fixed names; event bodies and visitor IDs stay out.\n\n## Count backend work separately\n\nCollect a bounded set of domain outcomes in a request-local `Ref`, then send one\nrequest-completed event and those outcomes together. Define whether health,\npreflight, discovery and streamed responses count. Pick a truthful completion\npoint for streams and cancellation; do not count a stream twice or claim the\nbody was delivered merely because response headers exist.\n\nUse native Effect `HttpClient` for capture rather than adding a server SDK solely\nto send batches. Check and encode the owned wire Schema; distinguish accepted\nHTTP responses, rejection and transport failure. Bound the entire send and keep\nresources scoped. Use Effect Clock/Random for timing and event identity; do not\nintroduce ambient clocks or random globals. Worker CPU timing can differ from\nprovider-measured latency, so name the recorded duration accurately.\n\nOn native Alchemy Cloudflare Workers, use the installed\n`WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)` API for bounded background sends.\nIt extends request work and preserves the native Effect context without a new\nrunner in owned code. Call it while the native request context is valid. The\ninstalled Alchemy implementation captures the calling services; it does not\nextend every service's acquisition scope. Close over only dependencies that\nremain alive for the send and open the HTTP send scope inside the background\nEffect. Do not carry a soon-to-close request scope or raw host context into a\nsecond runtime. Test request finalisation while the controlled send is pending.\nIt is not a persistent queue or a promise of delivery after termination.\nExpected analytics failure should not change the application's response when\nbest-effort loss is the agreed policy. Keep fixed safe diagnostics and separate\nbackend request IDs from browser visitor IDs. Do not add retries without deciding\nhow duplicates are prevented and how long sends may live.\n\n## Keep a browser relay on the owning Website\n\nFor a minimal browser client, an existing Website path such as `/ingest/e/` avoids\nanother Worker and cross-origin delivery to `api.<domain>`. A thin TanStack route\ncalls a native Effect HTTP program through the existing server runner. This is\nfixed provider ingress for the Website's browser client, not a domain API or a\nsecond Content/HTTP/RPC implementation. It is the narrow exception to keeping\nbackend machine routes out of the Website. A separate proxy Worker or API route\nis valid when isolation or other requirements justify it; decide that explicitly.\n\nSet the browser SDK's checked `api_host` to the Website path. Backend capture\ncan stay direct to its fixed regional ingestion host. The relay reduces direct\nanalytics-domain blocking even for explicit events; it cannot recover events\nthat never reach it or defeat every blocker. Preserve chosen privacy controls.\n\nQualify the pinned SDK's actual endpoint, media types, compression, query keys,\nbatch delay, retry policy and response handling. The source-derived SDK used\n`/e/`, roughly three-second batches, gzip bytes labelled `text/plain`, plus JSON\nand form fallbacks. Those are version-specific observations, not universal\nPostHog endpoints. Enabling flags, replay or external scripts introduces paths\nthat this minimal relay deliberately does not support; revisit the protocol\nbefore enabling them. Do not proxy arbitrary URLs or guessed assets.\n\nKeep the relay small but check its real boundary:\n\n- Fixed POST path, fixed regional upstream and allowed Website origin. Origin\n  checking is a browser policy, not authentication for a public project token.\n- Checked retry/compression query, rejecting duplicates and unknown keys.\n  Use Schema optional-to-Option codecs and `Option.match`; encode query keys\n  only at the outgoing boundary.\n- Unchanged bytes, UUIDs and timestamps. Do not parse and rebuild SDK batches\n  merely to forward them. The owning Schema checks protocol metadata and bounded\n  bytes; the browser consumer separately owns allowed event properties.\n- Native `Stream.limitBytes` bounds both request and reply even without\n  Content-Length. Supply a typed failing fallback when the limit is crossed:\n  `Stream.empty` would silently accept a truncated body. Test the exact limit\n  and the crossing chunk; never forward a partially collected oversized batch.\n  No manual mutable byte counter. Compressed-byte limits do not establish\n  decompressed event size; the forwarding relay does not decompress.\n- One scoped deadline covers request body, upstream headers and reply body.\n  The source pattern chose 1 MiB per body and five seconds; choose limits for the\n  target application's actual payloads and runtime constraints.\n- Rebuild allowed headers. Strip caller cookies, auth, IP forwarding and trace\n  headers; disable native HttpClient trace propagation. Configure the native\n  transport for manual redirects before sending, then reject every 3xx response\n  without forwarding its Location. Rejecting a final status is insufficient if\n  the transport has already followed it. In the qualified FetchHttpClient,\n  `RequestInit` is read at execution and its Layer uses `layerMergedContext` to\n  carry acquisition settings into requests. Providing manual redirects to that\n  Layer is supported; verify the actual call retains that setting and no caller\n  overrides it. Do not add `followRedirects`.\n- Preserve upstream success/error statuses and checked retry headers; return\n  `no-store`. Do not report a failed provider attempt as successful delivery.\n\nKeep SDK retries as the single retry owner for this design. Forward each attempt\nonce with no relay retry, durable queue or direct-send fallback unless explicitly\ndesigned. Browser batching still leaves a loss window on tab closure. Give relay\nrequests their own telemetry category so they do not inflate API/domain counts.\n\n## Verify the assembled capability\n\nTypecheck live and test implementations at the same closed service contract.\nCheck the error channel and Layer input/output types; a signature made clean by\nan assertion or `orDie` is not proof. Use `@effect/vitest` for Effect tests and\nEffect FileSystem/ChildProcess for test work; no raw async setup or real sleeps.\n\nUse the actual lint command and negative fixtures to reject SDK access outside\nthe browser adapter, Promise bridges outside named host adapters, misplaced\ncodecs, manual tag checks and unchecked optional extraction. Split large files\nby their schemas/service/error/transport owners; do not create forwarding helpers.\n\nTest unchanged bytes and supported formats, status handling, stripped headers,\nexact/oversized bodies without Content-Length, a stalled incoming body, late\nheaders followed by a stalled reply, interruption and resource cleanup. Native\nHttpClient may represent a bodyless response as a typed empty-body error; qualify\nand handle that case without masking other stream failures. Prove one upstream\nattempt and closed service requirements with deterministic Layers/TestClock.\nTest redirect refusal with a real local native transport as well as a fake\nHttpClient: a fake returning 302 cannot prove the real client never followed it.\nCover late completion after timeout, concurrent requests with separate state,\nrequest-scope closure during background sending and safe drop diagnostics.\n\nReject an implementation until enabled/disabled configuration, each named error,\nSchema round-trips, lifecycle, concurrency and the actual lint prohibitions have\nbeen checked. Missing live delivery evidence must remain a stated non-claim.\n\nThen follow the resource guide's delivery sequence: controlled enabled Preview,\nfresh browser requests, exact stored UUIDs and backend events, temporary domain\nblocking and client retries when relevant, restored privacy overrides and disabled\nordinary Preview, and separately authorised production plan/apply/readback.\nLocal tests, HTTP 200, provider state and stored-event queries are separate proof.\nKeep a dated receipt in the application repository; do not copy its account IDs,\npersonal details, tokens or deployment-specific findings into reusable skills.\n\nSee [PostHog proxy guidance](https://posthog.com/docs/advanced/proxy) and\n[native Alchemy Worker source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/tree/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers).\n# PostHog resources with native Alchemy\n\nUse this when adding PostHog to an Effect application or maintaining its projects,\ndashboards, insights and dashboard tiles. Management and event collection have\ndifferent owners and credentials. Read the sibling\n[application layout](../effect/posthog-analytics.md)\nand [browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md)\nfor the runtime side. Apply the sibling\n[strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to the management\nservice, provider callbacks and tests as well as the application.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Establish the intended collection](#establish-the-intended-collection)\n- [Separate resource management from capture](#separate-resource-management-from-capture)\n- [Declare durable resource ownership](#declare-durable-resource-ownership)\n- [Connect deployment outputs to the applications](#connect-deployment-outputs-to-the-applications)\n- [Prove each claim separately](#prove-each-claim-separately)\n- [Primary sources](#primary-sources)\n\n## Establish the intended collection\n\nAgree the questions and selected events before declaring charts. Keep browser\npage views, chosen actions, backend requests and completed domain operations\nseparate. A server request is not a visit, and retries are not new user actions.\nDecide collection controls, identity, allowed properties, retention, environment\nseparation and accepted event loss. Explicit events with automatic capture and\nrecordings off are a useful starting point, not a universal product requirement.\nConsent is an application decision; never infer its legal requirements from\nthe application's country or copy another application's choice.\n\nResolve the authorised account, organisation, project region and project limit.\nUse the existing secret store. Choose management-key capabilities from the actual\noperations: project/dashboard/insight management and query readback are distinct\nfrom billing. Billing and spending limits remain separate unless deliberately\nowned; inspect each product's limit instead of assuming one account-wide cap.\nStop an apply rejected by entitlement rather than retrying the same creates.\n\nInspect installed Alchemy, Effect and `@distilled.cloud/posthog` types and locked\nupstream references. The source-derived pattern was qualified with Effect\n`4.0.0-rc.117`, Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, Distilled `1.0.0-rc.12` and browser SDK\n`1.435.6`. This is a historical compatibility snapshot, not a current-version\nrecommendation. Verify it again for the target repository. Do not copy patches\nor internal SDK endpoints without proving the installed contract.\n\n## Separate resource management from capture\n\n| Owner                                            | Work                               | Credential                                    |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Native Alchemy stack and private Distilled Layer | Project/chart lifecycle            | Redacted management key, deployment only      |\n| Checked runtime configuration                    | Matching project, region and stage | Public project token; omit management details |\n| Browser Effect adapter                           | Selected browser events            | Matching public project token                 |\n| Backend Effect HttpClient Layer                  | Checked event batches              | Matching public project token                 |\n\nDistilled's management API is not the browser event SDK or a capture client.\nIts operations already return Effect: do not wrap them in Promise adapters.\nKeep raw operations, credentials and provider payloads inside\n`management.adapter.layer.ts`. Public service operations take branded addresses\nand return checked application-owned state. Decode results immediately; map\nexpected failures to safe named errors. Never let a raw provider response or\nmanagement key reach a Worker, root loader, browser bundle or error log.\n\nThe service exposes closed `Effect.Effect<Success, PostHogManagementError, never>`\noperations implemented with named `Effect.fn`; acquisition failures stay in the\nLayer error channel. Use Schema-backed Config and `Redacted` for credentials.\nNo raw client accessor, generic request/callback or primitive address strings.\nDo not erase provider errors with `Effect.option` or `orDie`. Model only genuine\nnot-found as `Option.none()` and branch with `Option.match`. Expected errors\ncarry bounded safe codes/reasons, not raw responses, SDK errors or arbitrary\nCauses. Distilled's typed transport still needs application boundary decoding.\n\n## Declare durable resource ownership\n\nUse native provider Resources if the installed Alchemy version supplies the\nneeded lifecycle. Otherwise implement small custom Resources and Providers for\nprojects, dashboards, insights and tiles. Follow\n[custom resource semantics](custom-resources.md); native Alchemy CLI remains\nthe only plan/apply/destroy owner. Do not create a second provider-write script.\n\nKeep checked definitions in schemas, a dashboard catalogue in its own owner,\nthe semantic management service separate from its live adapter, and lifecycle\nproviders separate from the root graph. Add further files only for real owners.\nThe root composes Layers, state, configuration and named resource declarations.\nResource definitions and stored outputs use Schema-derived branded identities\nwith actual format/range bounds; branding an unrestricted string is insufficient.\nDeclare known insight-query shapes and bounded catalogue values, not unchecked\nJSON or a generic `unknown` query payload. Keep deployment and runtime error\nvocabularies separate so provider-management details cannot leak into capture.\nUse outputs for project-dependent charts and tiles so the graph orders them.\n\nNormalise Alchemy's optional previous/output values to Option once inside each\nprovider callback and use `Option.match`; translate back only if the native\ncallback contract requires `undefined`. Preserve Alchemy's native Effect-returning\ncallback types instead of introducing a Promise runtime or unchecked cast.\nFor unresolved Outputs, follow the installed `Diff`/`Output` contract rather\nthan treating a deferred value as already decoded. A provider operation's\nrequired host context is not an excuse to expose it on management methods.\n\nUse stable logical resource IDs and exact ownership markers. A similar name,\nthe first search result or a project ID supplied by a foreign account does not\nprove ownership. Read an owned candidate and verify organisation/project/marker\nbefore mutation. Reject ambiguous matches. Do not automatically adopt a default\nproject. Ownership-field changes require an explicit replacement decision.\n\nList Schemas must describe all valid list items, including unrelated unnamed\ninsights and dashboards. Decode nullable/absent names to `Option`, then use\n`Option.match` to filter candidates. Apply stricter managed-name and ownership\nchecks only after selection. Follow every checked pagination page; a first-page\nmiss is not proof of absence. Check each page and bound traversal by total\noperation time, item/page limits and repeated-cursor detection. A limit or broken\npagination is a named inventory failure, not absence that permits creation.\nDo not fetch an unchecked next URL or forward credentials to another host.\nPreserve unrelated tile memberships when updating\na chart. A tile is an association with its own identity, not a duplicate insight.\n\nRead must distinguish missing, forbidden, invalid, transient and foreign data.\nDo not turn every failure into absence. Bound deadlines and retries with Effect;\nfor an uncertain create, read by ownership before any further write. An automatic\nretry of a non-idempotent create can duplicate projects or charts. Keep sensitive\nprovider bodies out of public failures and plan receipts.\n\nDeclare retention separately for each resource. Retaining a project preserves\nits event history and does not prevent updating or removing owned dashboards,\ninsights and tiles. Use each API's actual soft-delete semantics and check the\ndeleted flag on readback. Reviving a soft-deleted object needs an update even\nwhen desired fields are unchanged. Do not recreate a retained missing project\nsilently. Support the installed CLI's bulk-destroy path as well as ordinary\nremoval; test both against retained projects and foreign objects.\n\nA display rename from test to preview should preserve the existing logical ID,\nprovider ID, capture token and ownership marker. Existing configuration markers\ncan remain `test` if their checked contract requires it. New projects may use\nPreview naming from the start. Renaming is not permission to replace history.\n\n## Connect deployment outputs to the applications\n\nA dedicated analytics stack can own one production project and one shared\nnonproduction project. It need not create a project for every ephemeral PR.\nThe application stack reads the matching project through native references and\noutputs. Checked stage configuration selects production, deliberate Preview\ncollection or disabled collection; local and ordinary Preview collection should\nfollow the agreed policy. Reject cross-stage or cross-region combinations.\n\nEncode the owning configuration Schema once for each explicit Worker binding.\nKeep fallible validation/encoding in the typed provider or configuration owner\nbefore publishing outputs. In beta.79, `Output.mapEffect` requires an Effect\nwhose error type is `never`; it cannot carry an expected codec/configuration\nfailure. Use it only for genuinely infallible work, or select an already checked,\nencoded binding with pure `Output.map`. Do not add `orDie`, an assertion or an\nAction solely to hide that type restriction. Check the installed contract again\nfor another Alchemy version. Qualify Alchemy's runtime\nbinding representation: native environment handling can parse JSON before an\napplication JSON-string codec sees it. In beta.79, a native packed Redacted\nmarker preserved the encoded string; other versions require their own readback\ntest. Redaction avoids accidental logs, but a browser project token remains\npublic by design. Never distribute the whole deployment environment.\n\n## Prove each claim separately\n\n- Deterministic provider tests: create, no-op, update, uncertain-write recovery,\n  pagination, unnamed foreign items, ownership refusal, tile membership,\n  soft deletion and retained project removal. Include malformed responses,\n  permission refusal, incomplete/cyclic pagination, redacted errors, deadlines,\n  interrupted operations and cleanup. Typecheck live/test Layers against the\n  same closed contract; use native Effect test transport and `@effect/vitest`.\n- Native plan: exact revision/stage, expected create/update/remove actions,\n  capacity and account checks. Apply only within the user's authorised scope.\n- Provider readback: exact projects, privacy settings, private dashboards,\n  insight queries and tile membership. A successful apply is not this proof.\n- Runtime: matching bindings and real browser/backend sends on an isolated,\n  explicitly enabled Preview before production rollout.\n- Stored data: selected UUIDs counted once and separate backend event types.\n  A capture return or HTTP success does not prove storage.\n- Restoration: disable deliberate Preview capture through Alchemy, verify no\n  SDK load or sends in a fresh browser, and clean up ephemeral resources.\n\nKeep desired-state plans separate from provider drift. A no-change plan and a\nsuccessful drift command can coexist with reported provider differences. Record\nthem rather than repairing unrelated resources. A bounded receipt keeps source\nidentity, environment, plan/apply/readback and live checks separate; no secrets,\nvisitor identifiers, raw payloads or personal account details enter this skill.\n\n## Primary sources\n\nReconcile current documentation with installed types:\n[PostHog API](https://posthog.com/docs/api),\n[projects](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/projects),\n[capture](https://posthog.com/docs/api/capture),\n[privacy](https://posthog.com/docs/privacy),\n[Distilled source](https://github.com/distilled-cloud/distilled),\n[Alchemy source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy) and\n[Effect source](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect).\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":6250},"formatted_output":"import \"@tanstack/react-start/server-only\";\nimport { CalculatorHostTelemetryLive } from \"@taxkit/api-rpc/host-telemetry\";\nimport { env } from \"cloudflare:workers\";\nimport { ConfigProvider, Layer, ManagedRuntime } from \"effect\";\n\nimport { WebsiteServerLive } from \"./live.server.layer\";\n\n// One server runner per loaded Worker module. Each incoming server function\n// supplies its request signal; no request constructs a Layer or another runner.\nexport const appRuntime = ManagedRuntime.make(\n  Layer.mergeAll(\n    WebsiteServerLive(env.TAXKIT_API).pipe(\n      Layer.provide(ConfigProvider.layer(ConfigProvider.fromUnknown(env)))\n    ),\n    CalculatorHostTelemetryLive(\"website\")\n  )\n);\nsed: apps/web/src/worker.ts: No such file or directory\n# PostHog analytics in a separate Website and API\n\nUse this when adding browser and backend analytics to the repository pattern.\nThis is an optional capability, not part of every generated scaffold. Start from\nthe questions, collection policy and accepted loss budget, then follow the\n[Alchemy resource guide](../providers/posthog.md) and\n[Effect browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md).\nKeep product analytics separate from request logs, traces and operational metrics.\nApply the sibling [strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to\nschemas, services, adapters, infrastructure and tests; the SDK does not grant a\nwhole analytics folder an exception.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Put each responsibility with its owner](#put-each-responsibility-with-its-owner)\n- [Make the Effect contract reviewable](#make-the-effect-contract-reviewable)\n- [Count backend work separately](#count-backend-work-separately)\n- [Keep a browser relay on the owning Website](#keep-a-browser-relay-on-the-owning-website)\n- [Verify the assembled capability](#verify-the-assembled-capability)\n\n## Put each responsibility with its owner\n\nAdapt these paths to the target repository; they are an ownership map rather\nthan a requirement to create every file:\n\n```text\npackages/analytics/src/\n  schemas.ts                 checked settings, event vocabulary and brands\n  errors.ts                  closed capture failures\n  service.ts                 named browser/backend operations\n  live.layer.ts              native backend HTTP capture\n  test.layer.ts              deterministic service implementation\npackages/infrastructure/src/posthog/\n  schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n  management.adapter.layer.ts  private Distilled operations and credentials\n  resources.ts / dashboards.ts  resource lifecycle and desired chart catalogue\n  bindings.egress.ts            checked stage-specific deployment outputs\napps/web/src/lib/analytics/\n  browser.adapter.layer.ts   private SDK lifecycle\n  capture.consumer.ts        synchronous checked before-send callback\n  relay/\n    schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\n    live.layer.ts            one native outbound HTTP attempt\n    body.ingress.ts          bounded byte reader\n    request.ingress.ts       checked Website HTTP request\n    response.egress.ts       native response adaptation\napps/api/src/\n  analytics.layer.ts         request-scoped collection and finalisation\nalchemy.posthog.run.ts       composition-only resource stack\n```\n\nPackages import explicit package exports and never app internals. The existing\nserver runtime composes the relay service; the React-owned Atom runtime composes\nthe browser service. The native API entry composes backend capture with its\nincoming request Effect fibre. No analytics package starts a runtime or shares\nmutable per-request state across users.\n\n## Make the Effect contract reviewable\n\n- Public operations return `Effect.Effect<Success, AnalyticsError, never>` with\n  Schema-derived inputs and outputs. Capture transport, configuration and SDK\n  dependencies in the Layer; typecheck the real implementation against\n  `Service.of`. Native request handlers may retain documented host requirements;\n  those requirements must not leak into the shared analytics service.\n- Use named `Effect.fn(\"Service.operation\")` with a generator directly for\n  sequential public work. Use `Effect.fnUntraced` for reusable private work that\n  belongs to the caller's span. Keep pure total transformations plain. Split\n  files by schema/error/service/transport ownership, not one helper per SDK call.\n- Decode stage, host, token and collection mode with their owning Schema.\n  Model enabled/disabled settings as a closed union: disabled needs no fake key\n  or placeholder host. Invalid enabled settings are a named configuration\n  failure, not configured-off. If startup must remain available, make the\n  downgrade a reported policy at composition, separate from deliberate disable.\n- Decode external absence to `Option` once and prefer `Option.match` for both\n  branches. Do not access `.value`, inspect `_tag` or use unsafe extraction.\n  Use `Boolean.match` for boolean choices and `Match.exhaustive` for closed\n  states; do not introduce Option solely to hide a boolean decision. Return\n  nullable values only at a host contract that explicitly requires them.\n- Decode incoming data and encode owned outgoing payloads in their ingress and\n  egress owners. No assertions, raw JSON, raw fetch, ambient environment reads,\n  clocks, timers, random globals or raw Promise orchestration. The browser's\n  named SDK adapter and synchronous consumer are the documented host exceptions;\n  backend HttpClient and Distilled calls remain native Effect.\n- Map expected SDK/HTTP/codec failures to closed Schema-tagged errors with safe\n  operation/reason fields. Best-effort capture catches those named errors at\n  its application owner. Do not erase configuration, permission or decode\n  failure with blanket `Effect.option`/`Effect.ignore`, convert it with `orDie`,\n  or catch all Causes to report success. Preserve interruption and defects.\n  Discarding an invalid optional campaign field is a separate, explicit policy.\n- Create `Ref`s and any concurrency limits during the intended Layer/request\n  lifetime. Use scoped acquisition and bounded finalisers. No mutable module\n  client, request accumulator, per-click runner or detached fibre. Logging and\n  span fields use safe fixed names; event bodies and visitor IDs stay out.\n\n## Count backend work separately\n\nCollect a bounded set of domain outcomes in a request-local `Ref`, then send one\nrequest-completed event and those outcomes together. Define whether health,\npreflight, discovery and streamed responses count. Pick a truthful completion\npoint for streams and cancellation; do not count a stream twice or claim the\nbody was delivered merely because response headers exist.\n\nUse native Effect `HttpClient` for capture rather than adding a server SDK solely\nto send batches. Check and encode the owned wire Schema; distinguish accepted\nHTTP responses, rejection and transport failure. Bound the entire send and keep\nresources scoped. Use Effect Clock/Random for timing and event identity; do not\nintroduce ambient clocks or random globals. Worker CPU timing can differ from\nprovider-measured latency, so name the recorded duration accurately.\n\nOn native Alchemy Cloudflare Workers, use the installed\n`WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)` API for bounded background sends.\nIt extends request work and preserves the native Effect context without a new\nrunner in owned code. Call it while the native request context is valid. The\ninstalled Alchemy implementation captures the calling services; it does not\nextend every service's acquisition scope. Close over only dependencies that\nremain alive for the send and open the HTTP send scope inside the background\nEffect. Do not carry a soon-to-close request scope or raw host context into a\nsecond runtime. Test request finalisation while the controlled send is pending.\nIt is not a persistent queue or a promise of delivery after termination.\nExpected analytics failure should not change the application's response when\nbest-effort loss is the agreed policy. Keep fixed safe diagnostics and separate\nbackend request IDs from browser visitor IDs. Do not add retries without deciding\nhow duplicates are prevented and how long sends may live.\n\n## Keep a browser relay on the owning Website\n\nFor a minimal browser client, an existing Website path such as `/ingest/e/` avoids\nanother Worker and cross-origin delivery to `api.<domain>`. A thin TanStack route\ncalls a native Effect HTTP program through the existing server runner. This is\nfixed provider ingress for the Website's browser client, not a domain API or a\nsecond Content/HTTP/RPC implementation. It is the narrow exception to keeping\nbackend machine routes out of the Website. A separate proxy Worker or API route\nis valid when isolation or other requirements justify it; decide that explicitly.\n\nSet the browser SDK's checked `api_host` to the Website path. Backend capture\ncan stay direct to its fixed regional ingestion host. The relay reduces direct\nanalytics-domain blocking even for explicit events; it cannot recover events\nthat never reach it or defeat every blocker. Preserve chosen privacy controls.\n\nQualify the pinned SDK's actual endpoint, media types, compression, query keys,\nbatch delay, retry policy and response handling. The source-derived SDK used\n`/e/`, roughly three-second batches, gzip bytes labelled `text/plain`, plus JSON\nand form fallbacks. Those are version-specific observations, not universal\nPostHog endpoints. Enabling flags, replay or external scripts introduces paths\nthat this minimal relay deliberately does not support; revisit the protocol\nbefore enabling them. Do not proxy arbitrary URLs or guessed assets.\n\nKeep the relay small but check its real boundary:\n\n- Fixed POST path, fixed regional upstream and allowed Website origin. Origin\n  checking is a browser policy, not authentication for a public project token.\n- Checked retry/compression query, rejecting duplicates and unknown keys.\n  Use Schema optional-to-Option codecs and `Option.match`; encode query keys\n  only at the outgoing boundary.\n- Unchanged bytes, UUIDs and timestamps. Do not parse and rebuild SDK batches\n  merely to forward them. The owning Schema checks protocol metadata and bounded\n  bytes; the browser consumer separately owns allowed event properties.\n- Native `Stream.limitBytes` bounds both request and reply even without\n  Content-Length. Supply a typed failing fallback when the limit is crossed:\n  `Stream.empty` would silently accept a truncated body. Test the exact limit\n  and the crossing chunk; never forward a partially collected oversized batch.\n  No manual mutable byte counter. Compressed-byte limits do not establish\n  decompressed event size; the forwarding relay does not decompress.\n- One scoped deadline covers request body, upstream headers and reply body.\n  The source pattern chose 1 MiB per body and five seconds; choose limits for the\n  target application's actual payloads and runtime constraints.\n- Rebuild allowed headers. Strip caller cookies, auth, IP forwarding and trace\n  headers; disable native HttpClient trace propagation. Configure the native\n  transport for manual redirects before sending, then reject every 3xx response\n  without forwarding its Location. Rejecting a final status is insufficient if\n  the transport has already followed it. In the qualified FetchHttpClient,\n  `RequestInit` is read at execution and its Layer uses `layerMergedContext` to\n  carry acquisition settings into requests. Providing manual redirects to that\n  Layer is supported; verify the actual call retains that setting and no caller\n  overrides it. Do not add `followRedirects`.\n- Preserve upstream success/error statuses and checked retry headers; return\n  `no-store`. Do not report a failed provider attempt as successful delivery.\n\nKeep SDK retries as the single retry owner for this design. Forward each attempt\nonce with no relay retry, durable queue or direct-send fallback unless explicitly\ndesigned. Browser batching still leaves a loss window on tab closure. Give relay\nrequests their own telemetry category so they do not inflate API/domain counts.\n\n## Verify the assembled capability\n\nTypecheck live and test implementations at the same closed service contract.\nCheck the error channel and Layer input/output types; a signature made clean by\nan assertion or `orDie` is not proof. Use `@effect/vitest` for Effect tests and\nEffect FileSystem/ChildProcess for test work; no raw async setup or real sleeps.\n\nUse the actual lint command and negative fixtures to reject SDK access outside\nthe browser adapter, Promise bridges outside named host adapters, misplaced\ncodecs, manual tag checks and unchecked optional extraction. Split large files\nby their schemas/service/error/transport owners; do not create forwarding helpers.\n\nTest unchanged bytes and supported formats, status handling, stripped headers,\nexact/oversized bodies without Content-Length, a stalled incoming body, late\nheaders followed by a stalled reply, interruption and resource cleanup. Native\nHttpClient may represent a bodyless response as a typed empty-body error; qualify\nand handle that case without masking other stream failures. Prove one upstream\nattempt and closed service requirements with deterministic Layers/TestClock.\nTest redirect refusal with a real local native transport as well as a fake\nHttpClient: a fake returning 302 cannot prove the real client never followed it.\nCover late completion after timeout, concurrent requests with separate state,\nrequest-scope closure during background sending and safe drop diagnostics.\n\nReject an implementation until enabled/disabled configuration, each named error,\nSchema round-trips, lifecycle, concurrency and the actual lint prohibitions have\nbeen checked. Missing live delivery evidence must remain a stated non-claim.\n\nThen follow the resource guide's delivery sequence: controlled enabled Preview,\nfresh browser requests, exact stored UUIDs and backend events, temporary domain\nblocking and client retries when relevant, restored privacy overrides and disabled\nordinary Preview, and separately authorised production plan/apply/readback.\nLocal tests, HTTP 200, provider state and stored-event queries are separate proof.\nKeep a dated receipt in the application repository; do not copy its account IDs,\npersonal details, tokens or deployment-specific findings into reusable skills.\n\nSee [PostHog proxy guidance](https://posthog.com/docs/advanced/proxy) and\n[native Alchemy Worker source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy/tree/main/packages/alchemy/src/Cloudflare/Workers).\n# PostHog resources with native Alchemy\n\nUse this when adding PostHog to an Effect application or maintaining its projects,\ndashboards, insights and dashboard tiles. Management and event collection have\ndifferent owners and credentials. Read the sibling\n[application layout](../effect/posthog-analytics.md)\nand [browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md)\nfor the runtime side. Apply the sibling\n[strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to the management\nservice, provider callbacks and tests as well as the application.\n\n## Contents\n\n- [Establish the intended collection](#establish-the-intended-collection)\n- [Separate resource management from capture](#separate-resource-management-from-capture)\n- [Declare durable resource ownership](#declare-durable-resource-ownership)\n- [Connect deployment outputs to the applications](#connect-deployment-outputs-to-the-applications)\n- [Prove each claim separately](#prove-each-claim-separately)\n- [Primary sources](#primary-sources)\n\n## Establish the intended collection\n\nAgree the questions and selected events before declaring charts. Keep browser\npage views, chosen actions, backend requests and completed domain operations\nseparate. A server request is not a visit, and retries are not new user actions.\nDecide collection controls, identity, allowed properties, retention, environment\nseparation and accepted event loss. Explicit events with automatic capture and\nrecordings off are a useful starting point, not a universal product requirement.\nConsent is an application decision; never infer its legal requirements from\nthe application's country or copy another application's choice.\n\nResolve the authorised account, organisation, project region and project limit.\nUse the existing secret store. Choose management-key capabilities from the actual\noperations: project/dashboard/insight management and query readback are distinct\nfrom billing. Billing and spending limits remain separate unless deliberately\nowned; inspect each product's limit instead of assuming one account-wide cap.\nStop an apply rejected by entitlement rather than retrying the same creates.\n\nInspect installed Alchemy, Effect and `@distilled.cloud/posthog` types and locked\nupstream references. The source-derived pattern was qualified with Effect\n`4.0.0-rc.117`, Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, Distilled `1.0.0-rc.12` and browser SDK\n`1.435.6`. This is a historical compatibility snapshot, not a current-version\nrecommendation. Verify it again for the target repository. Do not copy patches\nor internal SDK endpoints without proving the installed contract.\n\n## Separate resource management from capture\n\n| Owner                                            | Work                               | Credential                                    |\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\n| Native Alchemy stack and private Distilled Layer | Project/chart lifecycle            | Redacted management key, deployment only      |\n| Checked runtime configuration                    | Matching project, region and stage | Public project token; omit management details |\n| Browser Effect adapter                           | Selected browser events            | Matching public project token                 |\n| Backend Effect HttpClient Layer                  | Checked event batches              | Matching public project token                 |\n\nDistilled's management API is not the browser event SDK or a capture client.\nIts operations already return Effect: do not wrap them in Promise adapters.\nKeep raw operations, credentials and provider payloads inside\n`management.adapter.layer.ts`. Public service operations take branded addresses\nand return checked application-owned state. Decode results immediately; map\nexpected failures to safe named errors. Never let a raw provider response or\nmanagement key reach a Worker, root loader, browser bundle or error log.\n\nThe service exposes closed `Effect.Effect<Success, PostHogManagementError, never>`\noperations implemented with named `Effect.fn`; acquisition failures stay in the\nLayer error channel. Use Schema-backed Config and `Redacted` for credentials.\nNo raw client accessor, generic request/callback or primitive address strings.\nDo not erase provider errors with `Effect.option` or `orDie`. Model only genuine\nnot-found as `Option.none()` and branch with `Option.match`. Expected errors\ncarry bounded safe codes/reasons, not raw responses, SDK errors or arbitrary\nCauses. Distilled's typed transport still needs application boundary decoding.\n\n## Declare durable resource ownership\n\nUse native provider Resources if the installed Alchemy version supplies the\nneeded lifecycle. Otherwise implement small custom Resources and Providers for\nprojects, dashboards, insights and tiles. Follow\n[custom resource semantics](custom-resources.md); native Alchemy CLI remains\nthe only plan/apply/destroy owner. Do not create a second provider-write script.\n\nKeep checked definitions in schemas, a dashboard catalogue in its own owner,\nthe semantic management service separate from its live adapter, and lifecycle\nproviders separate from the root graph. Add further files only for real owners.\nThe root composes Layers, state, configuration and named resource declarations.\nResource definitions and stored outputs use Schema-derived branded identities\nwith actual format/range bounds; branding an unrestricted string is insufficient.\nDeclare known insight-query shapes and bounded catalogue values, not unchecked\nJSON or a generic `unknown` query payload. Keep deployment and runtime error\nvocabularies separate so provider-management details cannot leak into capture.\nUse outputs for project-dependent charts and tiles so the graph orders them.\n\nNormalise Alchemy's optional previous/output values to Option once inside each\nprovider callback and use `Option.match`; translate back only if the native\ncallback contract requires `undefined`. Preserve Alchemy's native Effect-returning\ncallback types instead of introducing a Promise runtime or unchecked cast.\nFor unresolved Outputs, follow the installed `Diff`/`Output` contract rather\nthan treating a deferred value as already decoded. A provider operation's\nrequired host context is not an excuse to expose it on management methods.\n\nUse stable logical resource IDs and exact ownership markers. A similar name,\nthe first search result or a project ID supplied by a foreign account does not\nprove ownership. Read an owned candidate and verify organisation/project/marker\nbefore mutation. Reject ambiguous matches. Do not automatically adopt a default\nproject. Ownership-field changes require an explicit replacement decision.\n\nList Schemas must describe all valid list items, including unrelated unnamed\ninsights and dashboards. Decode nullable/absent names to `Option`, then use\n`Option.match` to filter candidates. Apply stricter managed-name and ownership\nchecks only after selection. Follow every checked pagination page; a first-page\nmiss is not proof of absence. Check each page and bound traversal by total\noperation time, item/page limits and repeated-cursor detection. A limit or broken\npagination is a named inventory failure, not absence that permits creation.\nDo not fetch an unchecked next URL or forward credentials to another host.\nPreserve unrelated tile memberships when updating\na chart. A tile is an association with its own identity, not a duplicate insight.\n\nRead must distinguish missing, forbidden, invalid, transient and foreign data.\nDo not turn every failure into absence. Bound deadlines and retries with Effect;\nfor an uncertain create, read by ownership before any further write. An automatic\nretry of a non-idempotent create can duplicate projects or charts. Keep sensitive\nprovider bodies out of public failures and plan receipts.\n\nDeclare retention separately for each resource. Retaining a project preserves\nits event history and does not prevent updating or removing owned dashboards,\ninsights and tiles. Use each API's actual soft-delete semantics and check the\ndeleted flag on readback. Reviving a soft-deleted object needs an update even\nwhen desired fields are unchanged. Do not recreate a retained missing project\nsilently. Support the installed CLI's bulk-destroy path as well as ordinary\nremoval; test both against retained projects and foreign objects.\n\nA display rename from test to preview should preserve the existing logical ID,\nprovider ID, capture token and ownership marker. Existing configuration markers\ncan remain `test` if their checked contract requires it. New projects may use\nPreview naming from the start. Renaming is not permission to replace history.\n\n## Connect deployment outputs to the applications\n\nA dedicated analytics stack can own one production project and one shared\nnonproduction project. It need not create a project for every ephemeral PR.\nThe application stack reads the matching project through native references and\noutputs. Checked stage configuration selects production, deliberate Preview\ncollection or disabled collection; local and ordinary Preview collection should\nfollow the agreed policy. Reject cross-stage or cross-region combinations.\n\nEncode the owning configuration Schema once for each explicit Worker binding.\nKeep fallible validation/encoding in the typed provider or configuration owner\nbefore publishing outputs. In beta.79, `Output.mapEffect` requires an Effect\nwhose error type is `never`; it cannot carry an expected codec/configuration\nfailure. Use it only for genuinely infallible work, or select an already checked,\nencoded binding with pure `Output.map`. Do not add `orDie`, an assertion or an\nAction solely to hide that type restriction. Check the installed contract again\nfor another Alchemy version. Qualify Alchemy's runtime\nbinding representation: native environment handling can parse JSON before an\napplication JSON-string codec sees it. In beta.79, a native packed Redacted\nmarker preserved the encoded string; other versions require their own readback\ntest. Redaction avoids accidental logs, but a browser project token remains\npublic by design. Never distribute the whole deployment environment.\n\n## Prove each claim separately\n\n- Deterministic provider tests: create, no-op, update, uncertain-write recovery,\n  pagination, unnamed foreign items, ownership refusal, tile membership,\n  soft deletion and retained project removal. Include malformed responses,\n  permission refusal, incomplete/cyclic pagination, redacted errors, deadlines,\n  interrupted operations and cleanup. Typecheck live/test Layers against the\n  same closed contract; use native Effect test transport and `@effect/vitest`.\n- Native plan: exact revision/stage, expected create/update/remove actions,\n  capacity and account checks. Apply only within the user's authorised scope.\n- Provider readback: exact projects, privacy settings, private dashboards,\n  insight queries and tile membership. A successful apply is not this proof.\n- Runtime: matching bindings and real browser/backend sends on an isolated,\n  explicitly enabled Preview before production rollout.\n- Stored data: selected UUIDs counted once and separate backend event types.\n  A capture return or HTTP success does not prove storage.\n- Restoration: disable deliberate Preview capture through Alchemy, verify no\n  SDK load or sends in a fresh browser, and clean up ephemeral resources.\n\nKeep desired-state plans separate from provider drift. A no-change plan and a\nsuccessful drift command can coexist with reported provider differences. Record\nthem rather than repairing unrelated resources. A bounded receipt keeps source\nidentity, environment, plan/apply/readback and live checks separate; no secrets,\nvisitor identifiers, raw payloads or personal account details enter this skill.\n\n## Primary sources\n\nReconcile current documentation with installed types:\n[PostHog API](https://posthog.com/docs/api),\n[projects](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/projects),\n[capture](https://posthog.com/docs/api/capture),\n[privacy](https://posthog.com/docs/privacy),\n[Distilled source](https://github.com/distilled-cloud/distilled),\n[Alchemy source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy) and\n[Effect source](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect).\n"},"started_at_ms":1791373035402,"completed_at_ms":1791373035402}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T11:37:15.448Z","ordinal":69537,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a11627-47b8-79b1-9f5a-05f505b92393","call_id":"call_Nix7dZjpDPBbN9tOb6SoRHEe","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 1.3 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"d3f7c4\",\"wall_time_seconds\":1.002664125,\"session_id\":28966,\"original_token_count\":0,\"output\":\"\"}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"ed1060\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.00000625,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6717,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 6717)\\nTotal output lines: 430\\n\\nimport \\\"@tanstack/react-start/server-only\\\";\\nimport { CalculatorHostTelemetryLive } from \\\"@taxkit/api-rpc/host-telemetry\\\";\\nimport { env } from \\\"cloudflare:workers\\\";\\nimport { ConfigProvider, Layer, ManagedRuntime } from \\\"effect\\\";\\n\\nimport { WebsiteServerLive } from \\\"./live.server.layer\\\";\\n\\n// One server runner per loaded Worker module. Each incoming server function\\n// supplies its request signal; no request constructs a Layer or another runner.\\nexport const appRuntime = ManagedRuntime.make(\\n  Layer.mergeAll(\\n    WebsiteServerLive(env.TAXKIT_API).pipe(\\n      Layer.provide(ConfigProvider.layer(ConfigProvider.fromUnknown(env)))\\n    ),\\n    CalculatorHostTelemetryLive(\\\"website\\\")\\n  )\\n);\\nsed: apps/web/src/worker.ts: No such file or directory\\n# PostHog analytics in a separate Website and API\\n\\nUse this when adding browser and backend analytics to the repository pattern.\\nThis is an optional capability, not part of every generated scaffold. Start from\\nthe questions, collection policy and accepted loss budget, then follow the\\n[Alchemy resource guide](../providers/posthog.md) and\\n[Effect browser adapter](../../../effect-client-wrapper/references/posthog-browser.md).\\nKeep product analytics separate from request logs, traces and operational metrics.\\nApply the sibling [strict Effect policy](../../../strict-effect-ts/SKILL.md) to\\nschemas, services, adapters, infrastructure and tests; the SDK does not grant a\\nwhole analytics folder an exception.\\n\\n## Contents\\n\\n- [Put each responsibility with its owner](#put-each-responsibility-with-its-owner)\\n- [Make the Effect contract reviewable](#make-the-effect-contract-reviewable)\\n- [Count backend work separately](#count-backend-work-separately)\\n- [Keep a browser relay on the owning Website](#keep-a-browser-relay-on-the-owning-website)\\n- [Verify the assembled capability](#verify-the-assembled-capability)\\n\\n## Put each responsibility with its owner\\n\\nAdapt these paths to the target repository; they are an ownership map rather\\nthan a requirement to create every file:\\n\\n```text\\npackages/analytics/src/\\n  schemas.ts                 checked settings, event vocabulary and brands\\n  errors.ts                  closed capture failures\\n  service.ts                 named browser/backend operations\\n  live.layer.ts              native backend HTTP capture\\n  test.layer.ts              deterministic service implementation\\npackages/infrastructure/src/posthog/\\n  schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\\n  management.adapter.layer.ts  private Distilled operations and credentials\\n  resources.ts / dashboards.ts  resource lifecycle and desired chart catalogue\\n  bindings.egress.ts            checked stage-specific deployment outputs\\napps/web/src/lib/analytics/\\n  browser.adapter.layer.ts   private SDK lifecycle\\n  capture.consumer.ts        synchronous checked before-send callback\\n  relay/\\n    schemas.ts / errors.ts / service.ts\\n    live.layer.ts            one native outbound HTTP attempt\\n    body.ingress.ts          bounded byte reader\\n    request.ingress.ts       checked Website HTTP request\\n    response.egress.ts       native response adaptation\\napps/api/src/\\n  analytics.layer.ts         request-scoped collection and finalisation\\nalchemy.posthog.run.ts       composition-only resource stack\\n```\\n\\nPackages import explicit package exports and never app internals. The existing\\nserver runtime composes the relay service; the React-owned Atom runtime composes\\nthe browser service. The native API entry composes backend capture with its\\nincoming request Effect fibre. No analytics package starts a runtime or shares\\nmutable per-request state across users.\\n\\n## Make the Effect contract reviewable\\n\\n- Public operations return `Effect.Effect<Success, AnalyticsError, never>` with\\n  Schema-derived inputs and outputs. Capture transport, configuration and SDK\\n  dependencies in the Layer; typecheck the real implementation against\\n  `Service.of`. Native request handlers may retain documented host requirements;\\n  those requirements must not leak into the shared analytics service.\\n- Use named `Effect.fn(\\\"Service.operation\\\")` with a generator directly for\\n  sequential public work. Use `Effect.fnUntraced` for reusable private work that\\n  belongs to the caller's span. Keep pure total transformations plain. Split\\n  files by schema/error/service/transport ownership, not one helper per SDK call.\\n- Decode stage, host, token and collection mode with their owning Schema.\\n  Model enabled/disabled settings as a closed union: disabled needs no fake key\\n  or placeholder host. Invalid enabled settings are a named configuration\\n  failure, not configured-off. If startup must remain available, make the\\n  downgrade a reported policy at composition, separate from deliberate disable.\\n- Decode external absence to `Option` once and prefer `Option.match` for both\\n  branches. Do not access `.value`, inspect `_tag` or use unsafe extraction.\\n  Use `Boolean.match` for boolean choices and `Match.exhaustive` for closed\\n  states; do not introduce Option solely to hide a boolean decision. Return\\n  nullable values only at a host contract that explicitly requires them.\\n- Decode incoming data and encode owned outgoing payloads in their ingress and\\n  egress owners. No assertions, raw JSON, raw fetch, ambient environment reads,\\n  clocks, timers, random globals or raw Promise orchestration. The browser's\\n  named SDK adapter and synchronous consumer are the documented host exceptions;\\n  backend HttpClient and Distilled calls remain native Effect.\\n- Map expected SDK/HTTP/codec failures to closed Schema-tagged errors with safe\\n  operation/reason fields. Best-effort capture catches those named errors at\\n  its application owner. Do not erase configuration, permission or decode\\n  failure with blanket `Effect.option`/`Effect.ignore`, convert it with `orDie`,\\n  or catch all Causes to report success. Preserve interruption and defects.\\n  Discarding an invalid optional campaign field is a separate, explicit policy.\\n- Create `Ref`s and any concurrency limits during the intended Layer/request\\n  lifetime. Use scoped acquisition and bounded finalisers. No mutable module\\n  client, request accumulator, per-click runner or detached fibre. Logging and\\n  span fields use safe fixed names; event bodies and visitor IDs stay out.\\n\\n## Count backend work separately\\n\\nCollect a bounded set of domain outcomes in a request-local `Ref`, then send one\\nrequest-completed event and those outcomes together. Define whether health,\\npreflight, discovery and streamed responses count. Pick a truthful completion\\npoint for streams and cancellation; do not count a stream twice or claim the\\nbody was delivered merely because response headers exist.\\n\\nUse native Effect `HttpClient` for capture rather than adding a server SDK solely\\nto send batches. Check and encode the owned wire Schema; distinguish accepted\\nHTTP responses, rejection and transport failure. Bound the entire send and keep\\nresources scoped. Use Effect Clock/Random for timing and event identity; do not\\nintroduce ambient clocks or random globals. Worker CPU timing can differ from\\nprovider-measured latency, so name the recorded duration accurately.\\n\\nOn native Alchemy Cloudflare Workers, use the installed\\n`WorkerExecutionContext.waitUntil(effect)` API for bounded background sends.\\nIt extends request work and preserves the native Effect context without a new\\nrunner in owned code. Call it while the native request context is valid. The\\ninstalled Alchemy implementation captures the calling services; it does not\\nextend every service's acquisition scope. Close over only dependencies that\\nremain alive for the send and open the HTTP send scope inside the background\\nEffect. Do not carry a soon-to-close request scope or raw host context into a\\nsecond runtime. Test request finalisation while the controlled send is pending.\\nIt is not a persistent queue or a promise of delivery after termination.\\nExpected analytics failure should not change the application's response when\\nbest-effort loss is the agreed policy. Keep fixed safe diagnostics and separate\\nbackend request IDs from browser visitor IDs. Do not add retries without deciding\\nhow duplicates are prevented and how long sends may live.\\n\\n## Keep a browser relay on the owning Website\\n\\nFor a minimal browser client, an existing Website path such as `/ingest/e/` avoids\\nanother Worker and cross-origin delivery to `api.<domain>`. A thin TanStack route\\ncalls a native Effect HTTP program through the existing server runner. This is\\nfixed provider ingress for the Website's browser client, not a domain API or a\\nsecond Content/HTTP/RPC implementation. It is the narrow exception to keeping\\nbackend machine routes out of the Website. A separate proxy Worker or API route\\nis valid when isolation or other requirements justify it; decide that explicitly.\\n\\nSet the browser SDK's checked `api_host` to the Website path. Backend capture\\ncan stay direct to its fixed regional ingestion host. The relay reduces direct\\nanalytics-domain blocking even for explicit events; it cannot recover events\\nthat never reach it or defeat every blocker. Preserve chosen privacy controls.\\n\\nQualify the pinned SDK's actual endpoint, media types, compression, query keys,\\nbatch delay, retry policy and response handling. The source-derived SDK used\\n`/e/`, roughly three-second batches, gzip bytes labelled `text/plain`, plus JSON\\nand form fallbacks. Those are version-specific observations, not universal\\nPostHog endpoints. Enabling flags, replay or external scripts introduces paths\\nthat this minimal relay deliberately does not support; revisit the protocol\\nbefore enabling them. Do not proxy arbitrary URLs or guessed assets.\\n\\nKeep the relay small but check its real boundary:\\n\\n- Fixed POST path, fixed regional upstream and allowed Website origin. Origin\\n  checking is a browser policy, not authentication for a public project token.\\n- Checked retry/compression query, rejecting duplicates and unknown keys.\\n  Use Schema optional-to-Option codecs and `Option.match`; encode query keys\\n  only at the outgoing boundary.\\n- Unchanged bytes, UUIDs and timestamps. Do not parse and rebuild SDK batches\\n  merely to forward them. The owning Schema checks protocol metadata and bounded\\n  bytes; the browser consumer separately owns allowed event properties.\\n- Native `Stream.limitBytes` bounds both request and reply even without\\n  Content-Length. Supply a typed failing fallback when the limit is crossed:\\n  `Stream.empty` would silently accept a truncated body. Test the exact limit\\n  and the crossing chunk; never forward a partially collected oversized batch.\\n  No manual mutable byte counter. Compressed-byte limits do not establish\\n  decompressed event size; the forwarding relay does not decompress.\\n- One scoped deadline covers request body, upstream headers and reply body.\\n  The source pattern chose 1 MiB per body and five seconds; choose limits for the\\n  target application's actual payloads and runtime constraints.\\n- Rebuild allowed headers. Strip caller cookies, auth, IP forwarding and tr…1217 tokens truncated…identity, allowed properties, retention, environment\\nseparation and accepted event loss. Explicit events with automatic capture and\\nrecordings off are a useful starting point, not a universal product requirement.\\nConsent is an application decision; never infer its legal requirements from\\nthe application's country or copy another application's choice.\\n\\nResolve the authorised account, organisation, project region and project limit.\\nUse the existing secret store. Choose management-key capabilities from the actual\\noperations: project/dashboard/insight management and query readback are distinct\\nfrom billing. Billing and spending limits remain separate unless deliberately\\nowned; inspect each product's limit instead of assuming one account-wide cap.\\nStop an apply rejected by entitlement rather than retrying the same creates.\\n\\nInspect installed Alchemy, Effect and `@distilled.cloud/posthog` types and locked\\nupstream references. The source-derived pattern was qualified with Effect\\n`4.0.0-rc.117`, Alchemy `2.0.0-beta.79`, Distilled `1.0.0-rc.12` and browser SDK\\n`1.435.6`. This is a historical compatibility snapshot, not a current-version\\nrecommendation. Verify it again for the target repository. Do not copy patches\\nor internal SDK endpoints without proving the installed contract.\\n\\n## Separate resource management from capture\\n\\n| Owner                                            | Work                               | Credential                                    |\\n| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |\\n| Native Alchemy stack and private Distilled Layer | Project/chart lifecycle            | Redacted management key, deployment only      |\\n| Checked runtime configuration                    | Matching project, region and stage | Public project token; omit management details |\\n| Browser Effect adapter                           | Selected browser events            | Matching public project token                 |\\n| Backend Effect HttpClient Layer                  | Checked event batches              | Matching public project token                 |\\n\\nDistilled's management API is not the browser event SDK or a capture client.\\nIts operations already return Effect: do not wrap them in Promise adapters.\\nKeep raw operations, credentials and provider payloads inside\\n`management.adapter.layer.ts`. Public service operations take branded addresses\\nand return checked application-owned state. Decode results immediately; map\\nexpected failures to safe named errors. Never let a raw provider response or\\nmanagement key reach a Worker, root loader, browser bundle or error log.\\n\\nThe service exposes closed `Effect.Effect<Success, PostHogManagementError, never>`\\noperations implemented with named `Effect.fn`; acquisition failures stay in the\\nLayer error channel. Use Schema-backed Config and `Redacted` for credentials.\\nNo raw client accessor, generic request/callback or primitive address strings.\\nDo not erase provider errors with `Effect.option` or `orDie`. Model only genuine\\nnot-found as `Option.none()` and branch with `Option.match`. Expected errors\\ncarry bounded safe codes/reasons, not raw responses, SDK errors or arbitrary\\nCauses. Distilled's typed transport still needs application boundary decoding.\\n\\n## Declare durable resource ownership\\n\\nUse native provider Resources if the installed Alchemy version supplies the\\nneeded lifecycle. Otherwise implement small custom Resources and Providers for\\nprojects, dashboards, insights and tiles. Follow\\n[custom resource semantics](custom-resources.md); native Alchemy CLI remains\\nthe only plan/apply/destroy owner. Do not create a second provider-write script.\\n\\nKeep checked definitions in schemas, a dashboard catalogue in its own owner,\\nthe semantic management service separate from its live adapter, and lifecycle\\nproviders separate from the root graph. Add further files only for real owners.\\nThe root composes Layers, state, configuration and named resource declarations.\\nResource definitions and stored outputs use Schema-derived branded identities\\nwith actual format/range bounds; branding an unrestricted string is insufficient.\\nDeclare known insight-query shapes and bounded catalogue values, not unchecked\\nJSON or a generic `unknown` query payload. Keep deployment and runtime error\\nvocabularies separate so provider-management details cannot leak into capture.\\nUse outputs for project-dependent charts and tiles so the graph orders them.\\n\\nNormalise Alchemy's optional previous/output values to Option once inside each\\nprovider callback and use `Option.match`; translate back only if the native\\ncallback contract requires `undefined`. Preserve Alchemy's native Effect-returning\\ncallback types instead of introducing a Promise runtime or unchecked cast.\\nFor unresolved Outputs, follow the installed `Diff`/`Output` contract rather\\nthan treating a deferred value as already decoded. A provider operation's\\nrequired host context is not an excuse to expose it on management methods.\\n\\nUse stable logical resource IDs and exact ownership markers. A similar name,\\nthe first search result or a project ID supplied by a foreign account does not\\nprove ownership. Read an owned candidate and verify organisation/project/marker\\nbefore mutation. Reject ambiguous matches. Do not automatically adopt a default\\nproject. Ownership-field changes require an explicit replacement decision.\\n\\nList Schemas must describe all valid list items, including unrelated unnamed\\ninsights and dashboards. Decode nullable/absent names to `Option`, then use\\n`Option.match` to filter candidates. Apply stricter managed-name and ownership\\nchecks only after selection. Follow every checked pagination page; a first-page\\nmiss is not proof of absence. Check each page and bound traversal by total\\noperation time, item/page limits and repeated-cursor detection. A limit or broken\\npagination is a named inventory failure, not absence that permits creation.\\nDo not fetch an unchecked next URL or forward credentials to another host.\\nPreserve unrelated tile memberships when updating\\na chart. A tile is an association with its own identity, not a duplicate insight.\\n\\nRead must distinguish missing, forbidden, invalid, transient and foreign data.\\nDo not turn every failure into absence. Bound deadlines and retries with Effect;\\nfor an uncertain create, read by ownership before any further write. An automatic\\nretry of a non-idempotent create can duplicate projects or charts. Keep sensitive\\nprovider bodies out of public failures and plan receipts.\\n\\nDeclare retention separately for each resource. Retaining a project preserves\\nits event history and does not prevent updating or removing owned dashboards,\\ninsights and tiles. Use each API's actual soft-delete semantics and check the\\ndeleted flag on readback. Reviving a soft-deleted object needs an update even\\nwhen desired fields are unchanged. Do not recreate a retained missing project\\nsilently. Support the installed CLI's bulk-destroy path as well as ordinary\\nremoval; test both against retained projects and foreign objects.\\n\\nA display rename from test to preview should preserve the existing logical ID,\\nprovider ID, capture token and ownership marker. Existing configuration markers\\ncan remain `test` if their checked contract requires it. New projects may use\\nPreview naming from the start. Renaming is not permission to replace history.\\n\\n## Connect deployment outputs to the applications\\n\\nA dedicated analytics stack can own one production project and one shared\\nnonproduction project. It need not create a project for every ephemeral PR.\\nThe application stack reads the matching project through native references and\\noutputs. Checked stage configuration selects production, deliberate Preview\\ncollection or disabled collection; local and ordinary Preview collection should\\nfollow the agreed policy. Reject cross-stage or cross-region combinations.\\n\\nEncode the owning configuration Schema once for each explicit Worker binding.\\nKeep fallible validation/encoding in the typed provider or configuration owner\\nbefore publishing outputs. In beta.79, `Output.mapEffect` requires an Effect\\nwhose error type is `never`; it cannot carry an expected codec/configuration\\nfailure. Use it only for genuinely infallible work, or select an already checked,\\nencoded binding with pure `Output.map`. Do not add `orDie`, an assertion or an\\nAction solely to hide that type restriction. Check the installed contract again\\nfor another Alchemy version. Qualify Alchemy's runtime\\nbinding representation: native environment handling can parse JSON before an\\napplication JSON-string codec sees it. In beta.79, a native packed Redacted\\nmarker preserved the encoded string; other versions require their own readback\\ntest. Redaction avoids accidental logs, but a browser project token remains\\npublic by design. Never distribute the whole deployment environment.\\n\\n## Prove each claim separately\\n\\n- Deterministic provider tests: create, no-op, update, uncertain-write recovery,\\n  pagination, unnamed foreign items, ownership refusal, tile membership,\\n  soft deletion and retained project removal. Include malformed responses,\\n  permission refusal, incomplete/cyclic pagination, redacted errors, deadlines,\\n  interrupted operations and cleanup. Typecheck live/test Layers against the\\n  same closed contract; use native Effect test transport and `@effect/vitest`.\\n- Native plan: exact revision/stage, expected create/update/remove actions,\\n  capacity and account checks. Apply only within the user's authorised scope.\\n- Provider readback: exact projects, privacy settings, private dashboards,\\n  insight queries and tile membership. A successful apply is not this proof.\\n- Runtime: matching bindings and real browser/backend sends on an isolated,\\n  explicitly enabled Preview before production rollout.\\n- Stored data: selected UUIDs counted once and separate backend event types.\\n  A capture return or HTTP success does not prove storage.\\n- Restoration: disable deliberate Preview capture through Alchemy, verify no\\n  SDK load or sends in a fresh browser, and clean up ephemeral resources.\\n\\nKeep desired-state plans separate from provider drift. A no-change plan and a\\nsuccessful drift command can coexist with reported provider differences. Record\\nthem rather than repairing unrelated resources. A bounded receipt keeps source\\nidentity, environment, plan/apply/readback and live checks separate; no secrets,\\nvisitor identifiers, raw payloads or personal account details enter this skill.\\n\\n## Primary sources\\n\\nReconcile current documentation with installed types:\\n[PostHog API](https://posthog.com/docs/api),\\n[projects](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/projects),\\n[capture](https://posthog.com/docs/api/capture),\\n[privacy](https://posthog.com/docs/privacy),\\n[Distilled source](https://github.com/distilled-cloud/distilled),\\n[Alchemy source](https://github.com/alchemy-run/alchemy) and\\n[Effect source](https://github.com/Effect-TS/effect).\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","create_time":1791373035.448481}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
