{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T11:44:36.211Z","ordinal":69640,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1162e-0173-7db2-8b58-613dde743647","call_id":"call_SdwMpPrWhHucRvkbhfH2TI3u","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 6.5 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"content: \"Found 6 relevant documentation page(s):\\n\\n# Organizations - Docs - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/settings/organizations\\n\\nhttps://posthog.com/docs/settings/organizations\\n\\n> Multiple organizations and projects aren't available on open source hobby deployments.\\n> \\n>\\n\\n\\n\\nhttps://posthog.com/docs/settings/organizations#organizations\\n\\n##  Organizations\\n\\n An organization is the highest abstraction level within a PostHog instance. It's made up of projects (see more in the [project settings guide](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/projects)) and members.\\n\\n Most commonly a PostHog organization represents a real-world company or other type of isolated grouping.\\n\\n Use the organization dropdown in the top bar to switch between organizations or create a new one.\\n\\n PostHog Cloud users can create, manage, and join organizations without limits.\\n\\n Organization members have different access levels based on their membership type. See more in our [access control docs](https://posthog.com/docs/settings/access-control).\\n\\n ###  Adding new members\\n\\n By default, any organization member can create and view organization invites. Admins and owners can restrict this by disabling **Members can invite** in organization settings – when disabled, only admins and owners can create and view invites. Members can only see invites at or below their own membership level. Invites are valid **for 3 days** after creation and **only for the specified email** . Emails can be resent by removing and re-adding the invitee.\\n\\n Sending invites is subject to [rate limits](https://posthog.com/docs/api#rate-limiting), which apply across your organization.\\n\\n In an [email-enabled PostHog instance](https://posthog.com/docs/self-host/configure/email) (including PostHog Cloud) the invite is sent automatically to the specified email. If the PostHog instance can't send emails, remember to share the invite link yourself.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Personal API keys - Docs - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/api/personal-api-keys\\n\\nhttps://posthog.com/docs/api/personal-api-keys\\n\\nPersonal API keys authenticate requests to PostHog's private `GET` , `POST` , `PATCH` , and `DELETE` endpoints. They're the right choice when you're using PostHog from your own scripts, automations, or any integration tied to your own account.\\n\\n> **Building an app that other PostHog users will install or connect to?** Use [OAuth](https://posthog.com/docs/api/oauth) instead so users can grant your app scoped access without sharing their own keys.\\n> \\n> \\n\\n  Personal API keys can enable full access to your account, like logging in with your email and password. You can create multiple, give them different scopes, and each can be invalidated individually. This improves the security of your PostHog account. Personal API keys need to be kept private and shouldn't be used in the frontend.\\n\\n ####  How to obtain a personal API key\\n\\n 1. Go to the [Personal API keys](https://us.posthog.com/settings/user-api-keys) section in your account settings\\n2. Click **+ Create a personal API Key** .\\n3. Give your key a label - this is just for you, usually to describe the key's purpose.\\n4. Choose the scopes for your key. We recommended selecting only the scopes required for the API endpoints you really need. This is a security best practice. You can always modify the scopes later if you need to.\\n5. At the top of the list, you should see your brand new key. **Immediately** copy its value, as you'll **never** see it again after refreshing the page.\\n\\n You can create up to 10 personal API keys. Personal API keys are deleted when a user is deleted.\\n\\n ![How to create an api key]() \\n\\n ####  Rolling and upgrading legacy keys\\n\\n Personal API keys created before February 2024 use legacy PBKDF2 hashing. These keys still work, but use an older, slower hashing method. They display a **Legacy** status tag in the keys table.\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Retrieve data from your endpoint - Docs - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data\\n\\n1. 1 ## Create an API key\\n\\n Required Endpoints support two kinds of keys, both with the `endpoint:read` scope.\\n\\n* **[Project secret API key](https://posthog.com/docs/api/project-secret-api-keys)** (beta, recommended for backend services): a project-scoped, server-to-server credential that isn't tied to a user. Create one in [Project secret API keys](https://app.posthog.com/settings/project-secret-api-keys). This is in beta and rolling out gradually – if the settings page isn't available in your organization yet, use a personal API key.\\n\\t* **[Personal API key](https://posthog.com/docs/api/personal-api-keys)** : scoped to your user account, useful for interactive or one-off scripts. Create one in [Personal API keys](https://app.posthog.com/settings/user-api-keys). To create a key:\\n\\n1. Open the relevant settings page above\\n\\t2. Click **Create key**\\n\\t3. Give it a name (e.g., \\\"Endpoints\\\")\\n\\t4. Select the `endpoint:read` scope\\n\\t5. Click **Create key** and copy it somewhere safe   **API keys are secret!** Store your API key securely. Never expose it in client-side code or commit it to version control.\\n2. 2 ## Choose your language\\n\\n Required Select your language or framework to see how to call your endpoint.\\n\\n* [cURL](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/api)\\n\\t* [![]() Python](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/python)\\n\\t* [![]() Node.js](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/nodejs)\\n\\t* [TypeScript](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/typescript)\\n\\t* [![]() Go](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/go)\\n\\t* [OpenAPI SDK](https://posthog.com/docs/endpoints/start-here/retrieve-data/openapi)\\n\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Organizations API Reference - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/api/organizations\\n\\n# Organizations\\n\\n> For instructions on how to authenticate to use this endpoint, see [API overview](https://posthog.com/docs/api/overview) .\\n> \\n> \\n\\n ### Endpoints\\n\\n|  |  |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/` |\\n| `POST` | `/api/organizations/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:id/` |\\n| `PATCH` | `/api/organizations/:id/` |\\n| `DELETE` | `/api/organizations/:id/` |\\n| `POST` | `/api/organizations/:id/remove_blocked_members_and_enforce_verified_domains/` |\\n| `POST` | `/api/organizations/:id/request_ai_access/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:id/teams/data_freshness/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/features/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/forecast/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/invoices/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/invoices/:invoice_id/content/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/limits/` |\\n\\n [Next page →](https://posthog.com/docs/api/organizations-2) ##  Retrieve\\n\\n #### Required API key scopes\\n\\n `organization:read` \\n\\n---\\n\\n #### Query parameters\\n\\n * limit integer\\n* offset integer\\n\\n---\\n\\n #### Response\\n\\n Show response body   \\n #### Example request\\n\\n `GET`   `/api  /organizations`  cURL  PostHog AI    \\n```\\nexport POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=[your personal api key]    curl \\\\        -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY\\\" \\\\        <ph_app_host>/api/organizations/\\n```\\n \\n```\\napi_key = \\\"[your personal api key]\\\"    project_id = \\\"[your project id]\\\"    response = requests.get(        \\\"<ph_app_host>/api/organizations/\\\".format(            project_id=project_id        ),        headers={\\\"Authorization\\\": \\\"Bearer {}\\\".format(api_key)},    ).json()\\n```\\n #### Example response\\n\\n ##### Status 200\\n\\n RESPONSE PostHog AI  \\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Members API Reference - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/api/members\\n\\n# Members\\n\\n> For instructions on how to authenticate to use this endpoint, see [API overview](https://posthog.com/docs/api/overview) .\\n> \\n> \\n\\n ### Endpoints\\n\\n|  |  |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/` |\\n| `PATCH` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/:user__uuid/` |\\n| `DELETE` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/:user__uuid/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/:user__uuid/github_login/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/:user__uuid/scoped_api_keys/` |\\n| `GET` | `/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/project_access/` |\\n\\n ##  List all members\\n\\n #### Required API key scopes\\n\\n `organization_member:read` \\n\\n---\\n\\n #### Query parameters\\n\\n * email_domain string\\n* levels string\\n* limit integer\\n* offset integer\\n* order string One of: `\\\"-joined_at\\\"` `\\\"joined_at\\\"`\\n* outside_verified_domains boolean\\n* search string\\n\\n---\\n\\n #### Response\\n\\n Show response body   \\n #### Example request\\n\\n `GET`   `/api  /organizations  /:organization_id  /members`  cURL  PostHog AI    \\n```\\nexport POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=[your personal api key]    curl \\\\        -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY\\\" \\\\        <ph_app_host>/api/organizations/:organization_id/members/\\n```\\n \\n```\\napi_key = \\\"[your personal api key]\\\"    project_id = \\\"[your project id]\\\"    response = requests.get(        \\\"<ph_app_host>/api/organizations/{organization_id}/members/\\\".format(            project_id=project_id        ),        headers={\\\"Authorization\\\": \\\"Bearer {}\\\".format(api_key)},    ).json()\\n```\\n #### Example response\\n\\n ##### Status 200\\n\\n RESPONSE PostHog AI  \\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n# Billing API Reference - PostHog\\nURL: https://posthog.com/docs/api/billing-4\\n\\n `GET`   `/api  /organizations  /:organization_id  /billing  /usage  /export`  cURL  PostHog AI    \\n```\\nexport POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=[your personal api key]    curl \\\\        -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY\\\" \\\\        <ph_app_host>/api/organizations/:organization_id/billing/usage/export/\\n```\\n \\n```\\napi_key = \\\"[your personal api key]\\\"    project_id = \\\"[your project id]\\\"    response = requests.get(        \\\"<ph_app_host>/api/organizations/{organization_id}/billing/usage/export/\\\".format(            project_id=project_id        ),        headers={\\\"Authorization\\\": \\\"Bearer {}\\\".format(api_key)},    ).json()\\n```\\n #### Example response\\n\\n ##### Status 200\\n\\n---\\n\\n ##  Retrieve billing usage status\\n\\n In beta: the organization billing API is behind the organization-billing-api feature flag and answers 403 without it, so ask support for access. Its shapes may change while it is in beta.\\n\\n #### Required API key scopes\\n\\n `billing:read` \\n\\n---\\n\\n #### Response\\n\\n Show response body   \\n #### Example request\\n\\n `GET`   `/api  /organizations  /:organization_id  /billing  /usage  /status`  cURL  PostHog AI    \\n```\\nexport POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY=[your personal api key]    curl \\\\        -H \\\"Authorization: Bearer $POSTHOG_PERSONAL_API_KEY\\\" \\\\        <ph_app_host>/api/o"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"b41ffd\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000006917,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":459,\"output\":\"web:build:   - import \\\"./docs-images.schemas\\\" without a file extension (scripts/docs-images.build.ts:9:39). Add the file extension\\nweb